{# PM Mode category page. Roadmap planner, sprint selection, power mode, and roadmap-sync shipped in the roadmap-power port (ported from ../erp, generalized). #}

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ I</p>
      <h2 id="claim">the pm work is in the session<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    </div>
    <p>Most agent setups treat product management as something that happens elsewhere: a ticket tracker, a planning doc, a standup call. The baseline treats it as part of the workflow, with the same rule: every step produces an artifact, and judgment stays in main context.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Requirements are captured before code is planned. Sprints carry machine-checkable done-criteria. The backlog closes atomically with the commit that picks an item up, enforced by the same hook that gates the commit itself. Cycle recaps and retrospectives read the repository, not your memory.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ II</p>
      <h2 id="toolkit">the shipped toolkit</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Seven capabilities ship today. Each names its mechanism; none of them is a prompt suggestion.</p>
  </div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>What it does</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="phase">brainstorm</td><td>Captures the requirement before any entry phase drafts an artifact. Derivation-first: it derives every field it can from context and asks at most two questions, only when the answer would change the build.</td><td>Constitution XI.3; probe cap enforced by <code>discipline.mjs</code>; brief lands at <code>docs/brief/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">standup</td><td>Reads release state, unreleased commits, backlog buckets, and open questions into one recap, then recommends the next pickup.</td><td>Deterministic gather + in-context recommendation; compact form at session start</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">backlog memory</td><td>Intent that survives sessions. Picked-up items are stamped closed in the same commit that lands the work.</td><td><code>backlog.md</code> stable keys; closure atomicity hard-blocked by <code>git_commit_guard</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">sprint-plan</td><td>Decomposes an MVP vision into a manifest where every feature carries done-criteria: a done-record, edge tests, a wiring test.</td><td>Manifest schema validated by <code>validateManifest</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">sprint-oracle</td><td>Refuses to call a sprint done until every feature is provably complete across all three dimensions.</td><td>Mechanical, exit-code-driven; fails loud with a per-feature gap list</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">commit-planner</td><td>Splits a heterogeneous working tree into single-concern Conventional Commits, read-only until the plan is approved.</td><td>Deterministic <code>inventory.mjs</code> grouping</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">retrospective</td><td>Turns a cycle's recurring friction into durable enforcement: landmine entries first, then graduation proposals up the funnel toward advisory hooks and hard gates.</td><td>Writes memory + a report; graduation requires the amendment path</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>An eighth, <code>whatsnew</code>, drafts keepachangelog-style entries as fragments the release pipeline consumes; it never writes the changelog itself.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ III</p>
      <h2 id="loop">one planning loop, enforced end to end</h2>
    </div>
    <p>The pieces compose into a cycle rather than a pile of commands.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Standup says where you are and what to pick up. Brainstorm captures the requirement into a brief. The workflow turns the brief into artifacts behind consent gates. The commit stamps the backlog closed, atomically. The retrospective converts whatever kept going wrong into structure, so the next cycle starts stronger. Every hop is either an artifact on disk or a hook that blocks, which is what separates PM Mode from a set of prompts.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ IV</p>
      <h2 id="roadmap">roadmap-driven delivery</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Three capabilities turn a decomposed roadmap into shipped work. All are live today; power mode is opt-in and off by default.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concepts is-row">
    <div class="concept">
      <p class="num">01 <span class="track-chip">roadmap-planner</span></p>
      <h3>Roadmap planner</h3>
      <p>Proves a roadmap's task <em>order</em> by re-deriving it from the vision and spec, then diffing that derivation against the existing plan. A deterministic graph engine finds cycles, dangling dependencies, and producer-after-consumer ordering violations, then emits a proposed order plus a delta report. It never blindly overwrites a hand-crafted roadmap. Two-lens WSJF scoring sequences the legal orders without crossing a hard dependency edge.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="concept">
      <p class="num">02 <span class="track-chip">sprint-planner</span></p>
      <h3>Sprint selection</h3>
      <p>Where <code>sprint-plan</code> decomposes a fresh vision, <code>sprint-planner</code> selects the next ready sprint from work you already decomposed. It reads the roadmap's machine-readable status, computes per-task readiness (ready when every dependency is done), orders with the planner's graph engine, and proposes a cohesive three-to-four ticket batch, excluding unready tasks and naming each unmet prerequisite. It proposes only. You confirm before <code>/triage</code> routes it, usually into power mode.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="concept">
      <p class="num">03 <span class="track-chip">power</span></p>
      <h3>Power mode</h3>
      <p>A batch-sprint track that delivers three or four related, spec-committed tickets in one cycle. The mechanical phases run once for the whole batch; judgment does not. Security review iterates per ticket, and a single ticket's blocker yields the batch exactly as a single-ticket workflow would. The batch lands as ordered Conventional Commits (build and config first, then implementation, tests, docs) with the closing workflow state on the final commit, enforced by the closure-atomicity guard. Opt-in via <code>velocity.power_mode.enabled</code>, off by default; requires git.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>Closing the loop, <code>roadmap-sync</code> runs as Phase 10.6 on every committing track. It flips the tasks a workflow just landed from ⬜ to ✅ in the project's roadmap and promotes their epic headings. It is fail-open: no roadmap or no matching task means a clean no-op, and it never blocks a commit.</p>
</section>

